WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.960 --> 00:00:09.010
- [Mark] All right, McKenzie.

00:00:09.010 --> 00:00:09.843
- Yes sir.

00:00:09.843 --> 00:00:10.676
- [Mark] McKenzie, where'd you grow up?

00:00:10.676 --> 00:00:11.509
Where are you from?

00:00:11.509 --> 00:00:12.342
- I'm from War.

00:00:12.342 --> 00:00:13.890
I growed up in John's Branch Holler.

00:00:13.890 --> 00:00:14.910
- [Mark] And that's West Virginia?

00:00:14.910 --> 00:00:15.743
- [McKenzie] Yes, sir.

00:00:15.743 --> 00:00:16.980
- [Mark] Not too far from here.

00:00:16.980 --> 00:00:21.900
- [McKenzie] Probably
about two, three miles.

00:00:21.900 --> 00:00:22.988
- [Mark] Oh, you're right down the street.

00:00:22.988 --> 00:00:24.187
And tell me about your family.

00:00:24.187 --> 00:00:26.550
You had both your parents
growing up as a kid?

00:00:26.550 --> 00:00:30.290
- Well, my mom and dad
divorced, I was at a young age,

00:00:30.290 --> 00:00:32.950
but I got to see my dad all the time.

00:00:32.950 --> 00:00:34.360
And my mom, she's still alive.

00:00:34.360 --> 00:00:35.553
My dad's still alive.

00:00:36.490 --> 00:00:38.523
And my brothers.

00:00:39.476 --> 00:00:40.840
- [Mark] How old are you?

00:00:40.840 --> 00:00:41.673
- I'm 28.

00:00:41.673 --> 00:00:42.540
- [Mark] Twenty-eight.

00:00:42.540 --> 00:00:44.390
Growing up as a young man in Appalachia,

00:00:44.390 --> 00:00:47.040
are there many other options
other than coal mining?

00:00:47.040 --> 00:00:50.200
- Well, I've done a number of things.

00:00:50.200 --> 00:00:53.320
I've been a police officer,

00:00:53.320 --> 00:00:56.400
a armed security guard at the
hospital over here at Welch.

00:00:56.400 --> 00:01:01.400
And I also helped take care of
the mentally disabled child.

00:01:02.130 --> 00:01:04.640
And, and then I come into the mines.

00:01:04.640 --> 00:01:07.540
- [Mark] Okay. So you've
done quite a few things.

00:01:07.540 --> 00:01:08.379
- Yes, sir.

00:01:08.379 --> 00:01:10.200
And do you enjoy mining?

00:01:10.200 --> 00:01:11.033
- I love it.

00:01:11.033 --> 00:01:12.090
- [Mark] You love it?

00:01:12.090 --> 00:01:13.885
What are your favorite things about it?

00:01:13.885 --> 00:01:16.150
- Just being with the
brothers underground.

00:01:16.150 --> 00:01:17.040
It's, it's a brotherhood.

00:01:17.040 --> 00:01:18.040
It's, it's a family.

00:01:18.040 --> 00:01:18.873
- [Mark] Uh-huh.

00:01:18.873 --> 00:01:21.190
- I mean, you just look
out for one another.

00:01:21.190 --> 00:01:24.283
And who I work for, he's a great guy.

00:01:27.213 --> 00:01:29.370
You can't, you can't
beat who you work for.

00:01:29.370 --> 00:01:30.203
- [Mark] Yeah.

00:01:30.203 --> 00:01:31.050
- And if you've got a good boss

00:01:31.050 --> 00:01:33.860
and you got to get crew, you mine coal.

00:01:33.860 --> 00:01:35.460
- [Mark] And it's a dangerous job,

00:01:35.460 --> 00:01:36.450
but you guys look out for each other.

00:01:36.450 --> 00:01:37.283
- Absolutely.

00:01:37.283 --> 00:01:38.116
- [Mark] Yeah.

00:01:38.116 --> 00:01:40.070
What, have you seen any kind of things

00:01:40.070 --> 00:01:44.370
happen on the job that, anyone get hurt?

00:01:44.370 --> 00:01:45.869
Or have you been hurt? Anything like that?

00:01:45.869 --> 00:01:48.570
- I haven't been hurt, per se.

00:01:48.570 --> 00:01:50.560
I had a rock, a piece of rock fell out

00:01:50.560 --> 00:01:54.150
and hit me in the head
a couple of weeks ago.

00:01:54.150 --> 00:01:59.120
But, it just cut my head a
little bit, a little pump knot.

00:01:59.120 --> 00:02:00.760
That was about it.

00:02:00.760 --> 00:02:01.723
No, I haven't,

00:02:03.000 --> 00:02:03.853
here, you know.

00:02:05.183 --> 00:02:07.580
- [Mark] Have there been
a lot of mine closures?

00:02:07.580 --> 00:02:08.413
- There's been a lot.

00:02:08.413 --> 00:02:09.246
- [Mark] Yeah.

00:02:09.246 --> 00:02:12.450
- There's, we're, we
are one of the lucky few

00:02:12.450 --> 00:02:13.400
that's still open.

00:02:13.400 --> 00:02:14.233
- [Mark] Yeah. It seems like,

00:02:14.233 --> 00:02:16.220
it seems like the coal industry is....

00:02:16.220 --> 00:02:18.630
- It's, it's slowly deteriorating.

00:02:18.630 --> 00:02:20.212
And around here, this is a way of life.

00:02:20.212 --> 00:02:21.430
- [Mark] And what's that gonna do

00:02:21.430 --> 00:02:23.470
to the economy of this region?

00:02:23.470 --> 00:02:25.670
I mean, for young men that
kind of relied on coal

00:02:25.670 --> 00:02:26.780
as a way to make a living?

00:02:26.780 --> 00:02:28.750
- It's gonna hurt. It's gonna hurt bad.

00:02:28.750 --> 00:02:30.560
Because this is what we know.

00:02:30.560 --> 00:02:32.320
This is all we do.

00:02:32.320 --> 00:02:34.640
We mine coal so we can support our family.

00:02:34.640 --> 00:02:37.370
- [Mark] Yeah, a lot of,
a lot of men like yourself

00:02:37.370 --> 00:02:39.790
have generations and generations of family

00:02:39.790 --> 00:02:40.901
that does this for a living.

00:02:40.901 --> 00:02:41.734
- Yeah.

00:02:41.734 --> 00:02:43.290
My grandfather done it.

00:02:43.290 --> 00:02:44.513
My uncles has done it.

00:02:48.210 --> 00:02:52.069
On my mom's side of the
family, her dad done it.

00:02:52.069 --> 00:02:53.573
Her uncles done it.

00:02:55.000 --> 00:02:58.370
And I'm the only one out of
me and my brother who does it.

00:02:58.370 --> 00:03:02.840
But, it's like I said,
it's just, it's slowly,

00:03:02.840 --> 00:03:04.012
it's slowly dyin.

00:03:04.012 --> 00:03:04.845
- [Mark] Yeah.

00:03:04.845 --> 00:03:09.000
- And people don't understand
that you've got to have coal.

00:03:09.000 --> 00:03:11.322
I mean, you have to, because coal it...

00:03:11.322 --> 00:03:16.322
your phones, women's
makeup, believe it or not,

00:03:16.681 --> 00:03:17.853
your vehicles.

00:03:18.993 --> 00:03:21.453
If you ain't got coal, you ain't got that.

00:03:23.140 --> 00:03:28.140
I mean, it's coal, is provided
for everything that you need.

00:03:32.290 --> 00:03:33.920
- [Mark] And you, do you
have a wife of your own?

00:03:33.920 --> 00:03:34.753
- Yes, I do.

00:03:34.753 --> 00:03:35.610
- [Mark] You do. And do you have children?

00:03:35.610 --> 00:03:36.443
- I have two.

00:03:36.443 --> 00:03:37.276
- [Mark] You have two.

00:03:37.276 --> 00:03:38.550
And what do you, what do you see,

00:03:38.550 --> 00:03:42.420
I mean, the future of the economy here?

00:03:42.420 --> 00:03:43.460
Like, do you have a son?

00:03:43.460 --> 00:03:44.882
- I have two, I have two boys.

00:03:44.882 --> 00:03:45.715
- [Mark] Two sons.

00:03:45.715 --> 00:03:47.340
I mean, what are the likelihood
they'll become coal miners

00:03:47.340 --> 00:03:48.173
like their dad?

00:03:49.552 --> 00:03:51.180
By the time they're old enough to work

00:03:51.180 --> 00:03:52.920
it may be a very different world.

00:03:52.920 --> 00:03:54.480
- [McKenzie] It will
probably be slim to none.

00:03:54.480 --> 00:03:55.960
But, I kind of, you know,

00:03:55.960 --> 00:03:58.130
I would like for them to experience

00:03:58.130 --> 00:03:59.140
what it's like underground,

00:03:59.140 --> 00:04:02.083
because it's, it's a totally
different world underground.

00:04:03.040 --> 00:04:07.270
And it's, I think they would enjoy it.

00:04:07.270 --> 00:04:08.270
I mean, it's hard work.

00:04:08.270 --> 00:04:09.430
It's an honest living.

00:04:09.430 --> 00:04:10.263
- [Mark] Yeah.

00:04:10.263 --> 00:04:13.010
- And, I love it.

00:04:13.010 --> 00:04:14.880
- [Mark] Yeah, it's a
very interesting job.

00:04:14.880 --> 00:04:18.265
I mean, the mines stay
around 60 degrees year round.

00:04:18.265 --> 00:04:19.830
Middle of winter, middle of summer,

00:04:19.830 --> 00:04:21.086
it's still 60 degrees down there, right?

00:04:21.086 --> 00:04:23.736
- Oh, Yeah. It's, it depends

00:04:23.736 --> 00:04:25.330
on what part of the mines you're at.

00:04:25.330 --> 00:04:27.580
Like, if you're going
toward like the intake,

00:04:28.620 --> 00:04:30.830
that's pretty much your
coldest part in the wintertime.

00:04:30.830 --> 00:04:31.780
- [Mark] Yeah.

00:04:31.780 --> 00:04:33.860
- And in the summertime, it feels great.

00:04:33.860 --> 00:04:34.757
- [Mark] Yeah, I'm sure.

00:04:34.757 --> 00:04:36.490
- I mean, if it's really
hot outside, and you're,

00:04:36.490 --> 00:04:37.397
you can just walk by the mines

00:04:37.397 --> 00:04:39.100
and you can just feel the air coming out.

00:04:39.100 --> 00:04:40.270
And it just feels like you're standing,

00:04:40.270 --> 00:04:41.520
like, right in front
of an air conditioner.

00:04:41.520 --> 00:04:42.353
- [Mark] A big one.

00:04:42.353 --> 00:04:43.770
- And then, in the
wintertime, you walk by it

00:04:43.770 --> 00:04:45.570
and it feels like you're
standing in front of a heater.

00:04:45.570 --> 00:04:46.958
- [Mark] Yeah.

00:04:46.958 --> 00:04:48.110
- [McKenzie] I mean, it's
probably about anywhere

00:04:48.110 --> 00:04:50.880
between 56 to 60 degrees year round.

00:04:50.880 --> 00:04:51.713
- [Mark] Year round.

00:04:51.713 --> 00:04:53.730
How did you go into the, into the earth?

00:04:53.730 --> 00:04:58.730
- [McKenzie] I work, I've been
as far back in as two miles.

00:04:59.050 --> 00:04:59.883
- [Mark] Two miles down.

00:04:59.883 --> 00:05:01.550
- [McKenzie] But here, we're
underground about a mile.

00:05:01.550 --> 00:05:02.633
- [Mark] Yeah.

00:05:02.633 --> 00:05:06.450
- [McKenzie] Anywhere
between 50 to 60 brakes.

00:05:06.450 --> 00:05:08.450
- [Mark] What's the most surprising thing

00:05:08.450 --> 00:05:10.860
that people outside of this world

00:05:10.860 --> 00:05:12.538
don't understand about?

00:05:12.538 --> 00:05:14.610
I mean, there's, there's
probably some interesting things

00:05:14.610 --> 00:05:16.560
about being down there
in the mine all day.

00:05:16.560 --> 00:05:17.560
You eat down there?

00:05:17.560 --> 00:05:18.393
- Oh, absolutely.

00:05:18.393 --> 00:05:19.520
- [Mark] Where do you go to the bathroom?

00:05:19.520 --> 00:05:20.353
- Uh,

00:05:21.410 --> 00:05:25.990
you go to the return or until a break.

00:05:25.990 --> 00:05:26.823
- [Mark] You just figure it out.

00:05:26.823 --> 00:05:28.716
- You just hope nobody sees ya, I guess.

00:05:28.716 --> 00:05:31.417
(both laughing)

00:05:31.417 --> 00:05:32.850
- [Mark] And you come
home filthy everyday,

00:05:32.850 --> 00:05:33.683
like you are now.

00:05:33.683 --> 00:05:34.516
- Every day.

00:05:34.516 --> 00:05:35.820
- [Mark] And you shower clean,

00:05:35.820 --> 00:05:38.420
and your, your, your squeaky
clean when you're done?

00:05:39.490 --> 00:05:40.510
- Some places, yeah.

00:05:40.510 --> 00:05:44.160
Well, you got your
eyes, sometimes, if you,

00:05:44.160 --> 00:05:45.980
you can scrub your eyes sometimes

00:05:45.980 --> 00:05:47.810
and it'll still stick to your eyes.

00:05:47.810 --> 00:05:49.490
And then your nails.

00:05:49.490 --> 00:05:51.300
Sometimes you got rough hands

00:05:51.300 --> 00:05:55.340
and it sticks in cracks
and crevices of your hands.

00:05:55.340 --> 00:05:56.750
But, yeah, nine times out of 10,

00:05:56.750 --> 00:05:58.370
you, you're gonna be clean.

00:05:58.370 --> 00:05:59.390
My wife don't like it

00:05:59.390 --> 00:06:00.680
'cause she's got to go clean the shower

00:06:00.680 --> 00:06:01.770
when I'm done showering. But....

00:06:01.770 --> 00:06:03.113
- [Mark] Oh, I'm sure, yeah.

00:06:05.570 --> 00:06:07.760
A lot of people from this
county, McDowell County,

00:06:07.760 --> 00:06:08.712
voted for Trump. Right?

00:06:08.712 --> 00:06:09.545
- Right.

00:06:09.545 --> 00:06:11.360
- [Mark] Did they, has he
helped the coal industry?

00:06:11.360 --> 00:06:12.193
- Absolutely.

00:06:13.182 --> 00:06:14.218
He really has.

00:06:14.218 --> 00:06:19.218
And, I think he will do
good again in another term.

00:06:19.810 --> 00:06:23.820
I think that we need his support.

00:06:23.820 --> 00:06:27.404
We need all of our Trump
supporters, to be honest with you.

00:06:27.404 --> 00:06:29.740
Because the more we get with Trump,

00:06:29.740 --> 00:06:31.700
Trump gets in again,

00:06:31.700 --> 00:06:33.390
coal mines will start to open again,

00:06:33.390 --> 00:06:34.840
coal will start boomin again.

00:06:35.770 --> 00:06:37.260
And it opens up more jobs.

00:06:37.260 --> 00:06:38.093
- [Mark] Yeah. It will help.

00:06:38.093 --> 00:06:38.926
- To people who need a job.

00:06:38.926 --> 00:06:40.580
- [Mark] Yeah. And in this
region, this region....

00:06:40.580 --> 00:06:42.440
- This region, there's
people that need a job.

00:06:42.440 --> 00:06:43.600
- [Mark] It depends on coal.

00:06:43.600 --> 00:06:44.433
- Exactly.

00:06:44.433 --> 00:06:47.210
And, like I said, around here,

00:06:47.210 --> 00:06:48.600
coal mining is the way of life.

00:06:48.600 --> 00:06:51.090
It's either that or flip a burger.

00:06:51.090 --> 00:06:54.400
And I don't much like fast food.

00:06:54.400 --> 00:06:55.233
- [Mark] Yeah.

00:06:55.233 --> 00:06:56.626
- So....

00:06:56.626 --> 00:06:58.020
- [Mark] What's your,
what's your favorite thing

00:06:58.020 --> 00:06:59.370
about living in Appalachia?

00:07:00.500 --> 00:07:01.570
- [McKenzie] I love the mountains.

00:07:01.570 --> 00:07:02.403
- [Mark] It's beautiful here.

00:07:02.403 --> 00:07:04.380
- [McKenzie] The mountains, the streams.

00:07:04.380 --> 00:07:07.170
It's, it's beautifulest place
in the world, I believe.

00:07:07.170 --> 00:07:09.070
- [Mark] It really is. Yeah.

00:07:09.070 --> 00:07:10.320
You're lucky to live here.

00:07:10.320 --> 00:07:11.590
- Absolutely, I love it.

00:07:11.590 --> 00:07:12.423
- [Mark] All right.

00:07:12.423 --> 00:07:13.730
- I couldn't go nowhere else.

00:07:13.730 --> 00:07:14.563
- [Mark] Excellent.

00:07:14.563 --> 00:07:15.750
McKenzie, thank you so
much for talking with me.

00:07:15.750 --> 00:07:16.583
- Thank you.

00:07:16.583 --> 00:07:17.443
- [Mark] Good luck, man.

